"A zero-trust policy diagram separates access decisions, policy administration, and enforcement around a protected resource.\n\nUse when: Review where dynamic access decisions are made and applied. Explain identity and device signals feeding session authorization.\n\nAvoid when: A static role-permission comparison is sufficient.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes distinguish subject, resource, policy engine, administrator, and enforcement point. Separate paths encode control-plane decisions and data-plane access. Signal arrows identify the evidence supplied to policy evaluation.\n\nBuild: Choose one resource-access scenario and its enforcement boundary. Identify decision and enforcement responsibilities. Draw identity, device, and other relevant signal inputs. Keep control and data paths in separate lanes with padded resource boundaries.\n\nCheck: Policy decisions have an actual enforcement point. Network location alone is not represented as sufficient trust. Signal freshness and unavailable-signal behavior are stated where relevant. Control arrows, access paths, and labels remain visually separated.\n\nPitfalls: Using the phrase zero trust as a substitute for concrete controls. Drawing a policy engine that cannot revoke or affect the depicted access path.\n\nExample: A workload request reaches an enforcement point, which obtains a policy decision informed by identity and device posture.\n\nAlternatives: Authorization graph, Network segmentation map."
